🌐 User Journey
A user types www.foobar.com in their browser.

The DNS system resolves www.foobar.com to the IP of the load balancer.

The load balancer (HAProxy) terminates SSL with the SSL certificate, ensuring encrypted traffic over HTTPS.

Traffic passes through firewalls before reaching backend servers.

The load balancer distributes requests between Server A and Server B, each running:

Web server (Nginx)

Application server

Codebase

Database (MySQL) (Primary on one, Replica on the other).

Monitoring clients collect metrics and logs from each server and send them to a centralized monitoring service (e.g., Sumo Logic).

🖥️ Additional Elements and Why They’re Added
3 Firewalls → One in front of the load balancer, and one protecting each backend server. They block unauthorized access and filter malicious traffic.

SSL certificate → Ensures traffic is encrypted, protecting user data and preventing eavesdropping.

3 Monitoring clients → Installed on each server to collect logs, metrics, and performance data for proactive troubleshooting.

📌 Roles Explained
Firewalls → Provide network security by filtering incoming/outgoing traffic based on rules.

HTTPS → Encrypts communication, ensuring confidentiality and integrity of data between user and server.

Monitoring → Tracks server health, performance, and security events.

Data collection → Monitoring agents send logs (web requests, errors, CPU usage, QPS) to a centralized service for analysis.

Monitoring QPS (Queries Per Second) → Configure the monitoring tool to track Nginx access logs or HAProxy stats, aggregating request counts per second.

⚠️ Issues with This Infrastructure
SSL termination at load balancer → Traffic between load balancer and backend servers may be unencrypted unless re‑encrypted, creating a potential vulnerability.

Only one MySQL server accepting writes → The Primary node is a bottleneck; if it fails, no writes can occur until failover is configured.

Servers with identical components → Running web, app, and database together on each server complicates scaling and resource allocation. Ideally, databases should be separated from web/app tiers.

